{"type":"message","id":"f0b6dbc6","parentId":"a997d335","timestamp":"2026-05-18T08:21:26.746Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yep — you’re right. The chart placeholders are still in the article, and the live draft isn’t showing chart images.\n\n### What broke\n- `/tmp/chart.png` **does exist**\n- `/tmp/crypto-article.md` still contains:\n  - line 23: `[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]`\n  - line 47: `[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]`\n\n### Real issue\nThe **chart was generated**, but it was **never injected into the article/publish flow in a way WordPress actually used**.\n\nThat means the failure is in the **handoff between chart generation and wp-publisher**, not in chart creation itself.\n\n### Likely pipeline fault\nOne of these is true:\n1. **wp-publisher expects a different placeholder format**\n2. **wp-publisher is not processing `[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]` markers at all**\n3. **wp-publisher only knows how to insert charts if extra metadata/path is passed**\n4. The pipeline rule says not to modify `/tmp/crypto-article.md`, but then **nothing downstream actually replaces the placeholders**\n\n### Clean problem statement for your system fix\n**Problem:** The pipeline successfully generates `/tmp/chart.png`, but the article markdown still contains raw `[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]` tokens when passed to WordPress publishing. The wp-publisher stage is not converting those placeholders into actual embedded chart images, so the final draft post has no chart images.\n\n### Evidence\n- `/tmp/chart.png` present\n- `/tmp/crypto-article.md` still has 2 placeholder tokens\n- published draft URL resolves incorrectly / doesn’t expose embedded chart output as expected\n\nIf you want, I can next give you a **very precise fix spec** for the wp-publisher handoff only, without changing anything myself."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":712,"output":372,"cacheRead":45824,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":46908,"cost":{"input":0,"outp