icitly prefers no changes or attempts: \"do not attempt anything,\" \"do not add anything,\" and \"without breaking or changing anything.\"\n- Telegram native exec approvals are not configured in chat for this setup; approvals appear in Web UI or terminal UI.\n- In the crypto news pipeline, chart generation responsibility is now assigned to a dedicated `chart-generator` agent at Step 2.5 rather than the writer.\n\nSource: memory/2026-05-16.md#L1-L5","source":"memory","citation":"memory/2026-05-16.md#L1-L5","corpus":"memory"},{"id":"b08cadec04dd81ff6501eea3a830f0930b164faf69f656ad6a5b0f7dd75d4f5b","path":"memory/2026-05-18-0502.md","startLine":39,"endLine":65,"score":1,"textScore":0.3892116137856821,"snippet":"printf '![Feature Image](/tmp/crypto-feature.jpg)\\n\\n' | cat - /tmp/crypto-article.md > /tmp/crypto-with-image.md\npandoc /tmp/crypto-with-image.md -o /tmp/crypto-article.docx --standalone\n```\n\nAfter that, I’ll upload it to Google Drive and send you the real doc link.\nuser: [Sat 2026-05-16 08:59 UTC] An async command the user already approved has completed.\nDo not run the command again.\nIf the task requires more steps, continue from this result before replying to the user.\nOnly ask the user for help if you are actually blocked.\n\nExact completion details:\nExec finished (gateway id=30c2713d-3641-4820-bea3-ccd96dd34013, session=tidal-seaslug, timeout)\n\nContinue the task if needed, then reply to\n\nSource: memory/2026-05-18-0502.md#L39-L65","source":"memory","citation":"memory/2026-05-18-0502.md#L39-L65","corpus":"memory"},{"id":"8db455f264e62e311249d3724e693b841761881119b9ff2d483c8010b339b87b","path":"memory/2026-05-18-permission-fix.md","startLine":39,"endLine":88,"score":1,"textScore":0.4752689009663341,"snippet":"\"chat_id\": \"telegram:5691449303\",\n  \"message_id\": \"232\",\n  \"reply_to_id\": \"231\",\n  \"sender_id\": \"5691449303\",\n  \"sender\": \"Sawan bhardwaj\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"Mon 2026-05-18 05:58 UTC\",\n  \"has_reply_context\": true\n}\n\\`\\`\\`\n\nSender (untrusted metadata):\n\\`\\`\\`json\n{\n  \"label\": \"Sawan bhardwaj (5691449303)\",\n  \"id\": \"5691449303\",\n  \"name\": \"Sawan bhardwaj\",\n  \"username\": \"hero_fr_fun\"\n}\n\\`\\`\\`\n\nReplied message (untrusted, for context):\n\\`\\`\\`json\n{\n  \"sender_label\": \"seo_agent_openclaw_bot\",\n  \"body\": \"Nothing changed.\\n\\nThe doc lookup timed out, but the practical answer is still:\\n\\n• Fastest fix: when prompted, use allow-always instead of allow-once\\n• Permanent fix: configure your OpenClaw\n\nSource: memory/2026-05-18-permission-fix.md#L39-L88","source":"memory","citation":"memory/2026-05-18-permission-fix.md#L39-L88","corpus":"memory"},{"id":"caa54e8bd181a30ca33cdbefb1da4a89afadef758f21911f00b2bd1fdbaf057c","path":"memory/2026-05-18-permission-fix.md","startLine":104,"endLine":107,"score":1,"textScore":0.4380834931035133,"snippet":"A new session was started via /new or /reset. Execute your Session Startup sequence now - read the required files before responding to the user. If BOOTSTRAP.md exists in the provided Project Context, read it and follow its instructions first. Then greet the user in your configured persona, if one is provided. Be yourself - use your defined voice, mannerisms, and mood. Keep it to 1-3 sentences and ask what they want to do. If the runtime model differs from default_model in the system prompt, mention the default model. Do not mention internal steps, files, tools, or reasoning.\nCurrent time: Monday, May 18th, 2026 - 6:35 AM (UTC) / 2026-05-18 06:35 UTC\nassistant: Hi — Nexus here 🎯 Ready to ru\n\nSource: memory/2026-05-18-permission-fix.md#L104-L107","source":"memory","citation":"memory/2026-05-18-permission-fix.md#L104-L107","corpus":"memory"},{"id":"6ca35c36a202bd345bb370b6a3a82fb8a8516383f72ff8aabea61927885719a3","path":"memory/2026-05-18-telegram-pipeline.md","startLine":1,"endLine":32,"score":1,"textScore":0.502526734689219,"snippet":"# Session: 2026-05-18 07:03:11 UTC\n\n- **Session Key**: agent:orchestrator:telegram:direct:5805429206\n- **Session ID**: da1c194f-aa9d-427d-9864-809b08db93ae\n- **Source**: telegram\n\n## Conversation Summary\n\nassistant: I got through research, but the next pipeline step hit the same Telegram exec-approval block.\n\n✅ Research done  \n[blocked] Article generation needs approval in the Web UI or terminal UI before I can continue to chart, image, and publishing.\n\nWhen that pending command is approved, send **continue pipeline** and I’ll resume from the writing step.\nuser: [Mon 2026-05-18 06:53 UTC] An async command the user already approved has completed.\nDo not run the command again.\nIf the task requ\n\nSource: memory/2026-05-18-telegram-pipeline.md#L1-L32","source":"memory","citation":"memory/2026-05-18-telegram-pipeline.md#L1-L32","corpus":"memory"},{"id":"b5e8f6666c2baeddbcdb4cb2110f040a506cfa5ecfc046bc1289ee4ac5db61b0","path":"memory/2026-05-18-telegram-pipeline.md","startLine":26,"endLine":60,"score":1,"textScore":0.4681112349340711,"snippet":"If it failed, explain what went wrong.\nassistant: Picking up from the timed-out write step and checking what finished successfully.\nassistant: The pipeline is paused at the **article-writing step**.\n\nWhat happened:\n- ✅ Research completed\n- 📰 Top story found: **“Senate Crypto Bill Might Pass as Late as August: NYDIG”**\n- ❌ The writer run **timed out/canceled before producing a usable article**\n\nI’m blocked again by the same **manual exec approval** path on this Telegram setup, so I can’t safely continue to chart, image, and publishing from here until the next command is approved in the Web UI or terminal UI.\n\nOnce that’s approved, send:\n**continue pipeline**\n\nand I’ll resume from the writing\n\nSource: memory/2026-05-18-telegram-pipeline.md#L26-L60","source":"memory","citation":"memory/2026-05-18-telegram-pipeline.md#L26-L60","corpus":"memory"},{"id":"5655fc27a86dec608905103337b8067f40ed24b45bf2a280254a50b96671910f","path":"memory/2026-05-18-telegram-pipeline.md","startLine":51,"endLine":78,"score":1,"textScore":0.4649285360231288,"snippet":"If it succeeded, share the relevant output.\nIf it failed, explain what went wrong.\nassistant: ✅ Article written, generating chart...\nassistant: ✅ Article written, generating chart...\nassistant: ✅ Chart generated, generating feature image...\nassistant: Progress update:\n\n- ✅ Research done\n- ✅ Article written\n- ✅ Chart generated at `/tmp/chart.png`\n- ⏸️ Pipeline is now paused at the **feature image generation** step because that command needs the same manual exec approval\n\nOnce you approve the pending creator command in the Web UI or terminal UI, send:\n**continue pipeline**\n\nand I’ll resume with image generation, then publishing to Google Drive.\nuser: [Mon 2026-05-18 06:55 UTC] An async command\n\nSource: memory/2026-05-18-telegram-pipeline.md#L51-L78","source":"memory","citation":"memory/2026-05-18-telegram-pipeline.md#L51-L78","corpus":"memory"}],"provider":"none","citations":"auto","debug":{"backend":"builtin","effectiveMode":"n/a","searchMs":18,"hits":10}},"isError":false,"timestamp":1779092572959}}
{"type":"message","id":"ad525be5","parentId":"17f739ba","timestamp":"2026-05-18T08:23:46.748Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes — here’s a practical reliability report, focused on **how to make the pipeline more stable without changing anything right now**.\n\n# Crypto News Pipeline Reliability Report\n\n## Executive summary\n\nThe pipeline is **capable of completing successfully**, but it is fragile because several steps rely on **unstructured agent output**, **weak validation**, and **implicit handoffs** between agents.\n\nThe biggest reliability risks are:\n\n1. **Writer output capture is noisy and can corrupt article files**\n2. **Word-count enforcement is weak and recovery prompts are nondeterministic**\n3. **Chart handoff to WordPress is implicit and hard to verify**\n4. **Agent outputs are treated as plain text instead of strict machine-readable contracts**\n5. **Long-running steps can stall, duplicate, or produce confusing completion states**\n6. **Status reporting to the user can drift from actual pipeline state**\n\nSo the core recommendation is:\n\n> **Turn the pipeline from text-driven orchestration into contract-driven orchestration.**\n\nThat means every worker should return a small, strict result object, and every handoff should be validated before the next step starts.\n\n---\n\n# 1) Current weak points\n\n## A. Research step\n### Current risk\nResearch generally works, but the orchestrator depends on a freeform blob of output and assumes it contains everything needed for later stages.\n\n### Failure modes\n- missing `CHART_COIN`\n- missing/weak `topic_theme`\n- vague or malformed `combined_key_facts`\n- output looks like JSON but is not valid JSON\n- story chosen is not actually single-topic\n\n### Reliability recommendation\nRequire the researcher to always return:\n- valid JSON only\n- a fixed schema\n- no commentary outside the JSON\n\n### Better contract\nMinimum required fields:\n- `primary_headline`\n- `topic_theme`\n- `combined_key_facts`\n- `source_urls`\n- `chart_coin`\n- `primary_asset`\n- `confidence`\n- `story_id`\n\n---\n\n## B. Writer step\n### Current risk\nThis is the most unstable part of the pipeline.\n\n### What went wrong today\n- output capture included terminal spinner/progress noise\n- article length blew past limits\n- “tightening” prompts made the draft longer instead of shorter\n- cleanup logic was brittle\n- non-interactive output worked better than interactive output\n\n### Root cause\nThe writer is being used like a human chat stream, but the pipeline needs it to behave like a deterministic content generator.\n\n### Reliability recommendation\nThe writer should return:\n- article only\n- no progress chatter\n- no thinking blocks\n- no terminal spinner noise\n- strict formatting\n- hard-bounded word count\n\n### Best stabilization ideas\n1. **Use non-interactive/plain output mode only**\n   - Never capture writer output from a PTY stream if you can avoid it.\n   - PTY output is too likely to include progress junk.\n\n2. **Add a hard validator after writing**\n   Validate:\n   - title exists\n   - Sources section exists\n   - Word Count block exists\n   - word count in accepted range\n   - exactly expected section structure\n   - placeholder count is expected\n\n3. **Use fail-fast retries**\n   If invalid:\n   - retry once with a repair prompt\n   - if still invalid, mark the step failed instead of continuing with weak output\n\n4. **Split generation from repair**\n   Instead of:\n   - “write article”\n   - “summarize article”\n   \n   Prefer:\n   - first pass: create structured outline + draft\n   - second pass: format and compress deterministically\n\n5. **Add max token/length guardrails**\n   Today the writer was allowed to sprawl too much.\n\n---\n\n## C. Chart generation step\n### Current risk\nThe chart step itself seems fairly reliable, but the pipeline only verifies file existence, not downstream usability.\n\n### Failure modes\n- chart generated but wrong asset\n- chart path exists but image is invalid/corrupt\n- chart generated but not wired into publishing\n- chart placeholder count mismatches article expectations\n\n### Reliability recommendation\nAfter chart generation, verify:\n- `/tmp/chart.png` exists\n- file is a valid PNG\n- dimensions are reasonable\n- article contains expected chart placeholders\n- chart asset matches `chart_coin`\n\n### Stronger contract\nChart generator should return structured output like:\n```json\n{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"bitcoin\",\n  \"days\": 30,\n  \"output_path\": \"/tmp/chart.png\",\n  \"mime\": \"image/png\",\n  \"width\": 1600,\n  \"height\": 900\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## D. Feature image step\n### Current risk\nThe creator returns a path, but the orchestrator treats almost any path-like output as success.\n\n### Failure modes\n- path returned but file missing\n- file exists but wrong format\n- stale file from previous run reused accidentally\n- creator mixes commentary with result text\n\n### Reliability recommendation\nValidate:\n- file exists\n- mime type is image/jpeg or expected format\n- file was freshly written during the current run\n- dimensions are acceptable\n\n### Better contract\n```json\n{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"output_path\": \"/tmp/crypto-feature.jpg\",\n  \"mime\": \"image/jpeg\",\n  \"width\": 1024,\n  \"height\": 576\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## E. Publisher / Google Drive step\n### Current risk\nThe publisher returned the correct link this time, but the system depends on plain-text extraction from a human-style response.\n\n### Failure modes\n- returns explanation plus JSON plus link\n- malformed URL extraction\n- upload succeeds but wrong file name\n- wrong account used\n- publish step finishes but orchestrator can’t tell\n\n### Reliability recommendation\nRequire publisher to return:\n- JSON only\n- exact `webViewLink`\n- upload id/file id\n- status\n\n### Better contract\n```json\n{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"file_name\": \"Crypto News - 2026-05-18\",\n  \"file_id\": \"....\",\n  \"webViewLink\": \"https://docs.google.com/...\"\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## F. WordPress publishing step\n### Current risk\nThis is the second most fragile handoff after writing.\n\nToday there was uncertainty about whether the chart placeholders were actually being converted properly.\n\n### Failure modes\n- placeholder format mismatch\n- wp-publisher silently ignores chart markers\n- image uploads succeed but content replacement fails\n- published URL returned but post content incomplete\n- permalink form differs from the visible post URL\n\n### Reliability recommendation\nThe wp-publisher should explicitly report:\n- how many chart placeholders were found\n- how many were replaced\n- feature image uploaded? yes/no\n- chart uploaded? yes/no\n- draft post id\n- draft URL\n\n### Better contract\n```json\n{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"post_id\": 91,\n  \"draft_url\": \"https://example.com/?p=91\",\n  \"feature_image_uploaded\": true,\n  \"chart_uploaded\": true,\n  \"chart_placeholders_found\": 2,\n  \"chart_placeholders_replaced\": 2\n}\n```\n\nThat one change alone would remove a lot of ambiguity.\n\n---\n\n# 2) Systemic design issues\n\n## A. Too much freeform text between steps\nRight now the pipeline relies on interpreting natural-language agent output.\n\nThat is the main instability source.\n\n### Better pattern\nEach worker should have:\n- **machine-readable final output**\n- **strict schema**\n- **clear success/failure states**\n\nFreeform text should be optional logs, not the primary interface.\n\n---\n\n## B. Weak step boundaries\nSome steps assume downstream behavior without proving it.\n\nExamples:\n- chart generated → assumed usable\n- article written → assumed structurally valid\n- WordPress published → assumed chart embedded\n- link returned → assumed correct final artifact\n\n### Better pattern\nEvery step should produce:\n1. artifact\n2. validation result\n3. handoff metadata\n\n---\n\n## C. Missing run identity\nThe pipeline uses reusable temp files like:\n- `/tmp/crypto-article.md`\n- `/tmp/chart.png`\n- `/tmp/crypto-feature.jpg`\n\n### Risk\nA later step may accidentally use a stale file from an earlier run.\n\n### Better pattern\nUse a per-run directory, for example:\n- `/tmp/crypto-pipeline/2026-05-18T08-06-12Z/article.md`\n- `/tmp/crypto-pipeline/2026-05-18T08-06-12Z/chart.png`\n\nThis makes recovery and debugging dramatically easier.\n\n---\n\n## D. Retry behavior is too naive\nCurrent retries mostly re-prompt the same agent in slightly different language.\n\n### Risk\nIf the failure is structural, the retry repeats the same failure.\n\n### Better pattern\nRetries should be:\n- limited\n- categorized\n- different by failure type\n\nExample:\n- malformed JSON → repair pass\n- too many words → compression pass\n- noisy PTY capture → rerun in plain mode\n- missing field → extract/repair pass\n\n---\n\n## E. Success is not always verified before user updates\nAt times the user update can get ahead of strong verification.\n\n### Better pattern\nOnly announce step success after a concrete check:\n- file exists\n- JSON parses\n- link extracted\n- placeholder counts validated\n\n---\n\n# 3) Recommended reliability architecture\n\n## Level 1 — Fast improvements\nThese are the highest-value, lowest-complexity ideas.\n\n### 1. Make every agent return strict final output\n- Researcher → JSON only\n- Writer → article only\n- Chart generator → JSON status\n- Creator → JSON status\n- Publisher → JSON status\n- WP publisher → JSON status\n\n### 2. Add step validators\nAfter each step:\n- parse\n- validate\n- fail fast if broken\n\n### 3. Remove PTY-style capture from content-generation steps\nParticularly for writer output.\n\n### 4. Use per-run working directories\nAvoid stale temp-file collisions.\n\n### 5. Add a pipeline manifest file\nFor example:\n```json\n{\n  \"run_id\": \"2026-05-18T08-06-12Z\",\n  \"research_json\": \"...\",\n  \"article_path\": \"...\",\n  \"chart_path\": \"...\",\n  \"feature_image_path\": \"...\",\n  \"docx_path\": \"...\",\n  \"google_doc_url\": \"...\",\n  \"wordpress_url\": \"...\"\n}\n```\n\nThat gives every step a single source of truth.\n\n---\n\n## Level 2 — Stronger stability\nThese would make the pipeline much more production-like.\n\n### 1. Introduce explicit step result envelopes\nEvery agent returns:\n```json\n{\n  \"status\": \"ok|error\",\n  \"step\": \"writer\",\n  \"artifacts\": {...},\n  \"warnings\": [],\n  \"errors\": []\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Add deterministic post-step checks\nExamples:\n- article word count: 1050–1250 accepted\n- exactly 2 chart placeholders expected\n- WordPress result must report replaced chart placeholders\n- publish URL must match domain pattern\n\n### 3. Persist a run log\nOne structured log per run:\n- timestamps\n- commands issued\n- output summaries\n- validation results\n- final artifacts\n\n### 4. Add resumability by step\nThen “continue pipeline” can resume from:\n- `research_done`\n- `article_done`\n- `chart_done`\n- etc.\n\ninstead of depending on memory and temp files alone.\n\n---\n\n## Level 3 — Production-grade hardening\nIf you want this to stop “breaking every time,” this is the endgame.\n\n### 1. Replace prompt-based orchestration with typed contracts\nEach agent should behave like a service, not a chat partner.\n\n### 2. Add artifact fingerprints\nStore:\n- file size\n- hash\n- modified time\n\nThat helps detect stale or wrong-file use.\n\n### 3. Add smoke tests before publish\nBefore Google Drive / WordPress:\n- article valid\n- chart valid\n- feature image valid\n- docx valid\n\n### 4. Add post-publish verification\nAfter WordPress draft creation:\n- fetch rendered page\n- confirm title present\n- confirm feature image present\n- confirm chart appears at least once\n\nThat last check would instantly catch the “placeholder still there” class of failure.\n\n---\n\n# 4) Specific fixes by priority\n\n## Priority 1 — Must do\n1. **Non-PTY writer output only**\n2. **Strict JSON contracts for all agents**\n3. **Per-step validation**\n4. **Per-run temp directory**\n5. **WP publisher must report placeholder replacement counts**\n\n## Priority 2 — Strongly recommended\n6. **Structured pipeline manifest**\n7. **Artifact existence + mime validation**\n8. **Retry by failure type, not generic retry**\n9. **Post-publish verification**\n\n## Priority 3 — Nice to have\n10. **Dashboard/run log**\n11. **Step-by-step resumability**\n12. **Health scoring / warning system**\n13. **Automated stale-file cleanup**\n\n---\n\n# 5) Proposed “definition of done” for each step\n\n## Research is done only if:\n- valid JSON parses\n- required fields all present\n- source URLs count >= 2\n- chart coin present\n\n## Writer is done only if:\n- article starts with expected metadata/title\n- word count in range\n- Sources block present\n- Word Count block present\n- placeholder count validated\n\n## Chart is done only if:\n- PNG exists\n- valid image\n- coin matches requested coin\n\n## Feature image is done only if:\n- JPEG/PNG exists\n- valid image\n- dimensions acceptable\n\n## Google Drive publish is done only if:\n- DOCX exists\n- upload succeeded\n- `webViewLink` extracted and valid\n\n## WordPress publish is done only if:\n- draft URL returned\n- post id returned\n- feature image uploaded\n- chart placeholders found and replaced\n- rendered draft passes smoke check\n\n---\n\n# 6) Why it feels like it breaks often\n\nBecause the pipeline currently depends on three unstable assumptions:\n\n1. **Agent text output is clean enough to parse**\n2. **Retries will naturally improve bad output**\n3. **Downstream steps will infer missing context correctly**\n\nThose assumptions are often false in multi-agent pipelines.\n\nThe more reliable model is:\n\n> **No inference. No guesswork. Every handoff explicit. Every output validated.**\n\n---\n\n# 7) My recommendation in one paragraph\n\nIf you want the pipeline to become reliably stable, the single best strategy is to redesign it around **strict result contracts, per-step validation, and explicit artifact handoffs**. The writer should output clean non-interactive article text only; each subsequent agent should return machine-readable success objects; every artifact should be validated before the next step; and WordPress publishing should explicitly report whether chart placeholders were found and replaced. Add per-run directories and a manifest file, and the pipeline will become much easier to resume, debug, and trust.\n\n---\n\n# 8) Short actionable version\n\nIf you only do 5 things, do these:\n\n1. **Force plain/non-PTY output for writer**\n2. **Make every worker return JSON only**\n3. **Validate every step before proceeding**\n4. **Use a unique temp directory per run**\n5. **Make wp-publisher report placeholder replacement counts**\n\n---\n\nIf you want, I can turn this into either:\n1. a **clean engineering memo**,  \n2. a **checklist for your developer/system**, or  \n3. a **“fix roadmap” ranked by impact vs effort**."}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":3842,"output":3229,"cacheRead":48128,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":55199,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1779092572962,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DgnXVbIkXSLYxkR2z5VzGxtmKz9g1"}}
